+# Feature Specification: JSON Pointer Resolution
+
+**Created**: 2026-10-21
+**Status**: Draft
+**Input**: User description: "Implement JSON Pointer (RFC 6901): export resolvePointer(document, pointer) from src/index.ts. Returns the value the pointer refers to; throws an error when the pointer is not a valid JSON Pointer, or when it does not resolve to a value of the document. First of several features that build on each other."
+
+## User Scenarios & Testing *(mandatory)*
+
+### User Story 1 - Read a value by pointer (Priority: P1)
+
+A service developer holds a JSON document and a JSON Pointer string and wants the value it designates.
+
+**Why this priority**: Core capability; later features build on it.
+
+**Independent Test**: Resolve pointers against the RFC 6901 section 5 example document and compare with the RFC's table.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** any document, **When** the pointer is the empty string, **Then** the whole document is returned.
+2. **Given** an object, **When** the pointer is `/foo`, **Then** the member `foo` is returned.
+3. **Given** an array, **When** the pointer is `/foo/0`, **Then** the first element is returned.
+4. **Given** keys `""`, `a/b`, `m~n`, **When** the pointers are `/`, `/a~1b`, `/m~0n`, **Then** the matching values are returned.
+5. **Given** a member whose value is `null`, `false`, `0` or `""`, **When** its pointer is resolved, **Then** that value is returned (not an error).
+
+---
+
+### User Story 2 - Reject invalid pointers (Priority: P1)
+
+A developer passing a malformed pointer gets an error, distinct from a valid pointer that finds nothing.
+
+**Why this priority**: Required by the request; protects callers from silent misuse.
+
+**Independent Test**: Call with malformed strings and verify an error is thrown.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** a non-empty pointer not starting with `/` (e.g. `foo`), **Then** an error is thrown.
+2. **Given** a `~` not followed by `0` or `1` (e.g. `/a~`, `/a~2`), **Then** an error is thrown.
+3. **Given** a pointer that is not a string, **Then** an error is thrown.
+
+---
+
+### User Story 3 - Reject pointers that resolve to nothing (Priority: P1)
+
+**Why this priority**: Required by the request; a missing value must never be confused with a real one.
+
+**Independent Test**: Resolve pointers naming absent members/indices and verify an error is thrown.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** an object without member `x`, **When** `/x` is resolved, **Then** an error is thrown.
+2. **Given** an array of length 2, **When** `/2` or `/-` is resolved, **Then** an error is thrown (`-` designates a nonexistent element).
+3. **Given** an array, **When** the token is not a valid index (`/01`, `/+1`, `/1.0`, `/-1`, `/a`), **Then** an error is thrown.
+4. **Given** a string, number, boolean or `null` reached before the pointer ends, **Then** an error is thrown.
+5. **Given** an object, **When** a token names an inherited property such as `/__proto__`, `/constructor` or `/toString`, **Then** an error is thrown.
+
+### Edge Cases
+
+- Tokens are unescaped by replacing `~1` with `/` first, then `~0` with `~`, so `~01` yields `~1`.
+- `/` refers to the member with the empty-string name, not the root.
+- Object member lookup is by exact, case-sensitive match; numeric-looking tokens on objects are plain member names.
+- Array indices are decimal with no leading zeros (except `0` itself).
+- The pointer is the RFC string representation only; URI-fragment form (`#/foo`) is not accepted.
+
+## Requirements *(mandatory)*
+
+### Functional Requirements
+
+- **FR-001**: The library MUST export `resolvePointer(document, pointer)` from `src/index.ts`.
+- **FR-002**: The empty-string pointer MUST return the whole document.
+- **FR-003**: A non-empty pointer MUST be a sequence of `/`-prefixed reference tokens; otherwise an error MUST be thrown.
+- **FR-004**: Within tokens, only `~0` and `~1` escapes are valid; any other `~` MUST cause an error.
+- **FR-005**: Tokens MUST be unescaped as `~1` then `~0`.
+- **FR-006**: On objects, a token MUST select the own member of that exact name; if absent, an error MUST be thrown.
+- **FR-007**: On arrays, a token MUST be a valid index (`0` or digits without leading zero) within bounds; otherwise (including `-`) an error MUST be thrown.
+- **FR-008**: Traversing into a primitive or `null` MUST cause an error.
+- **FR-009**: A resolved value that is falsy or `null` MUST be returned, not treated as missing.
+- **FR-010**: A non-string pointer MUST cause an error.
+- **FR-011**: The function MUST NOT modify the document and MUST return the referenced value itself, not a copy.
+
+### Key Entities
+
+- **Document**: any JSON value (object, array, string, number, boolean, null).
+- **Pointer**: a string identifying one value in a document by a path of reference tokens.
+
+## Success Criteria *(mandatory)*
+
+### Measurable Outcomes
+
+- **SC-001**: All examples of RFC 6901 section 5 and section 6 (string form) yield the specified values.
+- **SC-002**: 100% of the invalid-pointer and unresolvable-pointer cases listed above produce an error.
+- **SC-003**: Resolution never returns a value that is not part of the document.
+
+## Assumptions
+
+- Errors are ordinary thrown `Error` instances; no particular error subtype is required yet.
+- Inputs are JSON-compatible values already parsed; no JSON text parsing is done.
+- Resolving to `undefined` (e.g. a document containing an explicit `undefined` member) is out of scope.
+- Later features (e.g. updates) will reuse the parsing of pointers; this feature only reads.
